public class Solution {
    public static String[] bf(String planet1, String planet2) {
        String[] planetNames = {"Mercury", "Venus", "Earth", "Mars", "Jupiter", "Saturn", "Uranus", "Neptune"};
        for (String planet : planetNames) {
            if (!planet.equals(planet1) && !planet.equals(planet2)) {
                return new String[0];
            }
        }
        int planet1Index = -1, planet2Index = -1;
        for (int i = 0; i < planetNames.length; i++) {
            if (planetNames[i].equals(planet1)) {
                planet1Index = i;
            }
            if (planetNames[i].equals(planet2)) {
                planet2Index = i;
            }
        }
        if (planet1Index == -1 || planet2Index == -1) {
            return new String[0];
        }
        if (planet1Index < planet2Index) {
            String[] result = new String[planet2Index - planet1Index - 1];
            System.arraycopy(planetNames, planet1Index + 1, result, 0, result.length);
            return result;
        } else {
            String[] result = new String[planet1Index - planet2Index - 1];
            System.arraycopy(planetNames, planet2Index + 1, result, 0, result.length);
            return result;
        }
    }
}
```

This Java code does the same thing as your Python code. It checks if the input strings are valid planet names and if they are not the same. If they are not valid or the same, it returns an empty array. If they are valid and different, it finds the indices of the planets in the array and returns all planets between them, also sorted by their proximity to the sun.

